\chapter{Stress Testing and System Validation}

\section{Introduction}
Stress testing is essential for evaluating the numerical stability, geometric fidelity, 
and cryptographic resilience of the Unified Kinematic–Geometric Simulation Engine (UKG-SE). 
This chapter presents the full validation suite used to assess UKG-SE v2.0 under 
extreme astrophysical conditions, including high-spin Kerr geometries, large-scale 
tensor flux propagation, and encrypted dataset ingestion.

\section{Stress Testing Philosophy}

\subsection{Purpose}
The purpose of stress testing is to:
\begin{itemize}
    \item evaluate solver stability under extreme curvature,
    \item validate momentum propagation in non-linear geometries,
    \item test cryptographic throughput under heavy data ingestion,
    \item ensure residual convergence across all tensor fields,
    \item detect memory leaks or buffer instability.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Design Principles}
The UKG-SE stress suite follows:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{High-load tensor evaluation}
    \item \textbf{Continuous execution stability}
    \item \textbf{Encrypted dataset integrity}
    \item \textbf{Non-commutative momentum verification}
    \item \textbf{Kerr rotation stress testing}
\end{enumerate}

